= Holobiont =

== Overview ==
A '''holobiont''' is a host organism considered together with its associated community of symbiotic microorganisms. Depending on the research context, those associates can include bacteria, archaea, fungi, protists, and viruses.

The concept is useful because host phenotype, development, metabolism, immunity, ecology, and stress response can depend on interactions between the host and associated microbes. A holobiont is therefore an ecological and biological systems concept rather than a claim that every host–microbe association behaves as one perfectly integrated organism.

== Scope and boundaries ==
The word has broadened since its early use in symbiosis research. Modern literature commonly uses it for animals and plants together with their microbiota.

Several distinctions matter:

* '''host''' — the focal multicellular organism;
* '''microbiota''' — the microorganisms associated with a host or environment;
* '''microbiome''' — used variably for the microbial community, its collective genetic material, or both, depending on field and source;
* '''holobiont''' — host plus associated symbiotic community considered together;
* '''hologenome''' — the collective genetic information of the host and its microbial associates.

These terms describe different analytical objects and should not be treated as synonyms.

== Ecological organization ==
A holobiont contains interactions at several scales. Microbes may be intracellular or extracellular, persistent or transient, vertically or horizontally acquired, and beneficial, neutral, or harmful under different conditions.

The composition of a host-associated community can change with diet, environment, development, geography, disease, disturbance, and stochastic colonization. Consequently, a holobiont is not necessarily a fixed set of partner species.

This makes [[Related to::Systems biology|systems biology]], ecology, genomics, and network analysis relevant to holobiont research: the object of study includes interacting host and microbial processes rather than one genome in isolation.

== Evolutionary claims ==
The structural statement that hosts interact with associated microbial communities is distinct from the stronger '''hologenome theory of evolution''', which proposes that the holobiont can function as a unit of selection.

That stronger evolutionary claim is debated. Stable inheritance, partner fidelity, transmission mode, conflict among members, and the scale at which selection acts vary substantially among host–microbe systems. Evidence for an important microbial contribution to host biology does not by itself establish that every holobiont is an evolutionary individual.

== Research methods ==
Holobiont studies can combine:

* amplicon and metagenomic sequencing;
* host genomics and transcriptomics;
* metabolomics and proteomics;
* microscopy and spatial methods;
* culture and synthetic-community experiments;
* perturbation experiments;
* ecological and evolutionary modeling.

Interpretation requires controls for contamination, sampling compartment, sequencing and extraction bias, environmental covariates, and causal direction.

== Related concepts ==
[[Systems biology]], [[Genomics]], [[Ecosystem]], [[Complex system]], and [[System boundary]] provide useful neighboring frameworks. See also [[Holobiome]] for a related but less standardized term.

